<tr><th>Venue</th><td>Bridgestone Arena, Nashville</td></tr>
<tr><th>Channel</th><td>ABC and Disney+, streaming on Hulu the next day</td></tr>
<tr><th>Host</th><td>Lainey Wilson</td></tr>
<tr><th>Performance</th><td>Taylor Swift, first CMA Awards performance in 13 years</td></tr>
<tr><th>Nomination</th><td>Single of the Year for "I Knew It, I Knew You"</td></tr>
<tr><th>Song</th><td>Original song for Disney and Pixar's Toy Story 5, produced by Jack Antonoff, mixed by Laura Sisk</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>What happened</h2>
<p>The CMA and ABC announced the performance on Friday 13 November 2026 through Good Morning America. The announcement confirmed that Swift will take the stage at the 60th CMA Awards, but it did not say which song she will perform.</p>
<p>Her last appearance on the CMA Awards stage came in 2013, when she performed an acoustic version of "Red" alongside Vince Gill and Alison Krauss. That same year she received the Pinnacle Award, an honour given to an artist who has achieved global prominence through concert performances and record sales at levels unique in country music. She has not performed at the ceremony since.</p>
<p>The nomination she carries into this year's show is for "I Knew It, I Knew You", the original song she wrote for the soundtrack of Toy Story 5. The track was produced by Jack Antonoff, with Laura Sisk as mix engineer. It is her 26th career CMA nomination, and her first in four years.</p>
<p>The song has already made chart history of its own. It recently reached No. 1 on US country radio, her first song to top that list in 13 years. That run of success is part of what makes this year's performance such a talking point: the artist returning to the country awards stage is doing so with a country radio No. 1 behind her.</p>
<p>The announcement came just five days before the ceremony, leaving a short build-up window between the news and the night itself. The timing added to the excitement, with the confirmation arriving on a Friday and the show following on the Wednesday after.</p>
<p>Swift's connection to Nashville runs deep. She moved to the city as a teenager to pursue country music, signed her first record deal there, and built her early career on country radio. Her induction into the Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame on 26 October 2026 recognised that songwriting career, and this year marks the 20th anniversary of her self-titled debut album, released in 2006.</p>
<h2>Why it matters</h2>
<p>For the CMA Awards, the booking is a major one. The ceremony celebrates its 60th edition this year, and the return of one of the most famous artists in the world to its stage gives the night a headline moment. Swift's last performance at the show came before her full move into pop, so her return is framed as a homecoming as much as a performance.</p>
<p>For fans, the appeal is simple: a live performance from an artist who rarely plays awards shows, on a stage she has not played in 13 years, in the city where her career began. The timing adds to the occasion, coming just weeks after her Songwriters Hall of Fame induction and in the anniversary year of her debut album.</p>
<p>That anniversary is part of the story of the night. The debut album was released in 2006, so 2026 marks 20 years since it came out. Her induction into the Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame on 26 October 2026 fell in the same anniversary year, and the CMA Awards performance follows only a few weeks later. For an artist whose career started in Nashville, the three milestones landing in the same year make the return feel deliberate.</p>
<p>For country music, the moment also lands while a song she wrote for a family film tops country radio. Whether she performs the nominated song or something else, the performance puts the night's most-nominated talking point at the centre of the broadcast.</p>
<p>The ceremony itself is being hosted by Lainey Wilson, one of the leading names in current country music. The show airs live from Bridgestone Arena, the same venue that has hosted the CMA Awards for many years.</p>
<p>The 60th edition is a milestone in its own right. The CMA Awards have been running for six decades, and the ceremony remains the biggest night on the country music calendar. Bringing Swift back to the stage for the anniversary edition gives the show a headline performance to match the occasion.</p>
<h2>How to watch</h2>
<p>The 60th CMA Awards air live on Wednesday 18 November 2026 from Bridgestone Arena in Nashville, from 8 to 11 pm ET. Viewers in the United States can watch on ABC and on Disney+. The ceremony will be available to stream on Hulu the next day.</p>
<p>Fans outside the United States should check local listings, as broadcast arrangements differ by country. The show is hosted by Lainey Wilson.</p>
<p>For more on the night, read our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">latest news on Growentix</a>, where we will cover the results as they are announced.</p>
<h2>What is not announced yet</h2>
<p>Several details about the performance remain unconfirmed. The CMA and ABC have not announced which song Swift will perform. It is widely expected to be "I Knew It, I Knew You", the song for which she is nominated, but that has not been confirmed, and no other song has been named either.</p>
<p>No collaborators have been confirmed for the performance, and no details about staging have been made public. Swift is not part of the Brooks and Dunn tribute showcase at this year's ceremony.</p>
<p>Stay with this story for updates as the ceremony approaches. If the song or any special guests are confirmed before the show, the announcement will come from the CMA or ABC.</p>
